FEATURES             Location/Qualifiers
     terminator      1..72
                     /label="rrnBT1"
                     /synonym="rrnBT1"
                     /synonym="rrnBT1 terminator"
                     /synonym="rrnB T1"
                     /synonym="rrnB T1 terminator"
                     /synonym="T1"
                     /sub_region_of="rrnBT1T2"
                     /note="rrnB T1, the strong intrinsic (Rho-independent)
                     transcription terminator from the T1-T2 terminator cluster
                     downstream of the 5S rRNA gene in the E. coli rrnB
                     ribosomal RNA operon; 72 bp from the canonical rrnB operon
                     sequence J01695 (6366-6437; Brosius et al. 1981): from the
                     base after the 5S rRNA, which the record ends at 6365, to
                     the 3' end of the iGEM registry's T1 (BBa_B0010, 'T1 from
                     E. coli rrnB', J01695 6358-6437 plus two cloning bases). It
                     holds the A-rich stretch, the GC-rich hairpin, the U-tract
                     and the class II sequence. Cambray et al. 2013 (Suppl.
                     Table 1) measured T1 twice: the 31 bp hairpin and U-tract
                     alone ('rrnB T1 [min]', NC_000913.2 4169794-4169824, here
                     bases 15-45) at 74.3% termination, and T1 with its native
                     flanks ('rrnB T1', NC_000913.2 4169764-4169854, which holds
                     all of this part plus 16 bp of the 5S rRNA and 3 bp
                     downstream) at 97.3%. The 91 bases this part carried before
                     (J01695 6438-6528, toward T2) and the 6 bases of 5S rRNA at
                     its 5' end are documented by no source as part of T1.
                     Terminates host (E. coli sigma-70) RNA polymerase via a
                     GC-rich hairpin followed by a short U-tract; >97%
                     termination efficiency (Cambray et al. 2013). The element
                     additionally carries a class II conserved sequence
                     (TATCTGTT) that triggers termination by phage (T7/SP6) RNA
                     polymerases independently of the hairpin."
                     /db_xref="SO:0000141"
                     /citation=[1]
                     /citation=[5]
                     /citation=[8]
     stem_loop       19..41
                     /label="T1 GC-rich hairpin (class I intrinsic terminator
                     stem-loop)"
                     /parent="rrnBT1"
                     /note="23-nt GC-rich hairpin (transcript RNA
                     5'-CAGUCGAAAGACUGGGCCUUUCG-3'), the class I
                     (hairpin-dependent) intrinsic-termination signal of rrnB
                     T1. Lyakhov et al. 1997 defined the class I vs class II
                     signals of T1 and showed that disrupting this hairpin
                     abolishes class I termination — in the context of phage T7
                     RNA polymerase; Schwartz, Rahmouni & Boudvillain 2003
                     anatomized intrinsic-terminator hairpin contacts by NAIM
                     more generally. The hairpin's role in host E. coli RNAP
                     intrinsic termination at this exact stem is inferred from
                     the canonical intrinsic-terminator architecture rather than
                     directly mapped here. The hairpin is immediately followed
                     by the U-tract. Coordinates confirmed by exact sequence
                     match at 18-41."
                     /db_xref="SO:0000313"
                     /citation=[1]
                     /citation=[5]
                     /citation=[6]
                     /citation=[8]
     misc_feature    42..45
                     /label="poly-U tract (rrnB T1)"
                     /parent="rrnBT1"
                     /note="Four-uridine (UUUU) run in the transcript,
                     immediately 3' of the T1 hairpin, that destabilizes the
                     RNA:DNA hybrid to release the polymerase. The U-run is
                     unusually short (4 nt vs the 6-8 nt consensus); Schmidt &
                     Chamberlin 1987 showed NusA enhances rrnB T1 termination in
                     vitro to in-vivo-equivalent levels, and Orosz et al. 1991
                     confirmed T1 acts as an efficient standalone terminator in
                     a reporter assay. TTTT confirmed at 41-45."
                     /db_xref="SO:0000110"
                     /citation=[1]
                     /citation=[3]
                     /citation=[4]
     misc_feature    45..52
                     /label="class II conserved sequence (TATCTGTT) — phage RNAP
                     termination signal"
                     /parent="rrnBT1"
                     /note="8-bp conserved sequence (CS) TATCTGTT that is the
                     class II termination signal of rrnB T1, acting
                     independently of the upstream hairpin and relevant only for
                     phage RNA polymerases (T7, SP6). Lyakhov et al. 1997 showed
                     T7 RNAP mutants that fail at the class I hairpin still
                     terminate at this class II CS; Sohn & Kang 2004
                     characterized it mechanistically (sequential conformational
                     changes in T7 RNAP, no hairpin required). Not required for
                     E. coli RNAP intrinsic termination. TATCTGTT confirmed at
                     44-52."
                     /db_xref="SO:0000110"
                     /citation=[5]
                     /citation=[7]
